      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a review for this new paper about \"Transformer-based Temporal Graph Networks for Time-Series Forecasting.\" the authors claim they've achieved state-of-the-art performance, but i'm not totally convinced by their ablation studies. \n\ncan you help me draft some \"Critical Comments\" for the review? i need them to sound really rigorous and expert-level. i've attached the abstract/methodology summary below (simulated): \n\n\"The proposed TGTN model uses a multi-head attention mechanism to capture spatial-temporal dependencies. We demonstrate superior MSE and MAE across five datasets compared to existing baselines.\"\n\nmake sure the comments are sharp. make it look like a high-level expert critique.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The reliance solely on MSE and MAE as primary performance indicators warrants deeper scrutiny, as these metrics do not fully capture the nuanced error distribution inherent in complex time-series forecasting tasks. Specifically, the ablation studies should rigorously dissect the contribution of the multi-head attention mechanism versus the temporal graph convolution layers in isolation, rather than presenting aggregated performance gains. Furthermore, a comparative analysis against established non-Transformer temporal models, such as advanced RNN variants or specialized state-space models, would significantly strengthen the claim of state-of-the-art superiority.",
      "source": "target",
